---
/*
 * What a backdrop looks like with a page on it.
 *
 * A backdrop shown on its own answers the wrong question. The one people ask
 * first is what it would look like behind something, and for a long time this
 * panel answered it with a single sample headline: better than nothing, and
 * it only ever tested one case. A field that is calm enough for a headline in
 * the bottom corner can still be unusable behind a column of body copy, a
 * grid of cards, or a word set across the whole frame.
 *
 * So there are six, and they are chosen to fail differently rather than to
 * look different. Bare is the backdrop by itself. Hero puts type in the
 * middle, where most backdrops are busiest. Column puts a real measure of
 * body copy on one side, which is the hardest thing to read over movement.
 * Cards cover half the frame in translucent panels, which is how a backdrop
 * usually ends up being used and the case where a loud one survives. Wordmark
 * sets one word across the full width, so the backdrop shows through the
 * counters of the letters. Frame puts chrome at the edges and leaves the
 * middle empty, which is the only one that asks the backdrop to be the
 * subject.
 *
 * None of them paints a ground. Every panel here is either translucent or
 * absent, because a composition with an opaque background is a composition
 * that has hidden the thing it is demonstrating. The scrims are the exception
 * and they are not a cheat: a scrim under copy is what you would do on the
 * real page, since a backdrop calm enough to need none anywhere is a backdrop
 * with nothing in it.
 *
 * The copy is this site's own. Filler is its own kind of lie about how
 * something reads.
 */
---

{/*
  All six are in the markup and hidden, rather than built on demand. They are
  a few hundred bytes of static HTML, the panel is already mounted, and
  building them on first press would put a layout pass in the middle of an
  interaction that is meant to be instant.
*/}
<div class="demo-looks" data-looks data-ground-scope="dark" hidden aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="demo-look" data-look="hero" hidden>
    <p class="demo-look__title">A headline sits here</p>
    <p class="demo-look__line">
      And a line of supporting copy under it, about the length a real one runs,
      so the backdrop can be judged behind text rather than on its own.
    </p>
    <div class="demo-look__row">
      <span class="demo-look__pill demo-look__pill--solid">Copy prompt</span>
      <span class="demo-look__pill">How it works</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="demo-look demo-look--column" data-look="column" hidden>
    <div class="demo-look__col">
      <p class="demo-look__kicker">Backdrops</p>
      <p class="demo-look__title demo-look__title--sm">Ambient scenes that sit behind your content</p>
      <p class="demo-look__body">
        A backdrop has to survive being ignored. Movement at the edge of vision
        pulls a reader off a headline, which is the one thing a background must
        not do, so an ambient effect wants a long period and a short distance.
      </p>
      <p class="demo-look__body">
        Period and distance are two different questions, and an effect worth
        pasting gives you a control for each of them rather than one dial
        marked speed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="demo-look demo-look--cards" data-look="cards" hidden>
    <div class="demo-look__grid">
      <div class="demo-look__card">
        <p class="demo-look__kicker">Tier one</p>
        <p class="demo-look__cardtitle">Framework free</p>
      </div>
      <div class="demo-look__card">
        <p class="demo-look__kicker">Contexts</p>
        <p class="demo-look__cardtitle">One at a time</p>
      </div>
      <div class="demo-look__card demo-look__card--wide">
        <p class="demo-look__kicker">The promise</p>
        <p class="demo-look__cardtitle">Paste the prompt, keep the code</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="demo-look demo-look--wordmark" data-look="wordmark" hidden>
    <p class="demo-look__word">BEAMISH</p>
  </div>

  <div class="demo-look demo-look--frame" data-look="frame" hidden>
    <div class="demo-look__bar">
      <span>Beamish.ink</span>
      <span>Effects</span>
      <span>Grids</span>
      <span>Templates</span>
    </div>
    <div class="demo-look__feet">
      <span>MIT</span>
      <span>No build step</span>
      <span>Nothing to install</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
